          "bbox": [
            0.211,
            0.083,
            0.883,
            0.14
          ],
          "content": "7. A bar magnet has no markings to indicate its poles. How would you find out near which end its North pole is located without using another magnet?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.211,
            0.148,
            0.883,
            0.188
          ],
          "content": "8. If the earth is itself a magnet, can you guess the poles of earth’s magnet by looking at the direction of the magnetic compass?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.211,
            0.195,
            0.655,
            0.306
          ],
          "content": "9. While a mechanic was repairing a gadget using a screw driver, the steel screws kept falling down. Suggest a way to solve the problem of the mechanic on the basis of what you have learnt in this chapter."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.212,
            0.314,
            0.655,
            0.442
          ],
          "content": "10. Two ring magnets X and Y are arranged as shown in Fig. 4.16. It is observed that the magnet X does not move down further. What could be the possible reason? Suggest a way to bring the magnet X in contact with magnet Y, without pushing either of the magnets."
        },
        {
